    notes: "This port's own: ember-rust's address is the installer's `--domain`, passed on as `TLS_DOMAIN`, where a Worker answers at whichever addresses Cloudflare routes to it. Ember holds no Cloudflare token, which attaching the domain itself would take, so the administrator attaches it in Cloudflare's dashboard, and Ember takes only the address the page is open at, so it never sends people to one that doesn't reach it. The app moves pages, rather than the Worker redirecting them, as it learns the domain with the rest of the workspace (`/api/bootstrap`): nothing is cached, and Stop using it takes effect at once. Sessions and push subscriptions belong to their address, so people sign in once more at the custom domain and turn notifications on again there. The server can't tell which of someone's subscriptions are one browser's, as a user agent is shared by every Mac on the same version of Chrome, so the page that moves names its own; a browser that reaches the custom domain without moving there is pushed at both addresses until a page at the old one moves it. HSTS leaves out ember-rust's `includeSubDomains` for an apex domain's sake (`platform.security-headers`). A Worker can't read its own name, so the steps take it from the workers.dev address the page is open at, which is where a deploy puts Ember before it has a domain. Check DNS asks from the Worker, since the page's own policy only lets it reach Ember's address, and a probe proves the domain reaches this Ember's Workspace, not just a Worker, so a domain added to another Ember answers \"Something else\". The Workspace keeps the latest 20 probes in memory for 30 seconds. Links to Ember in messages, which open in the app and get no preview, are still the ones to the address the page is open at, since Ember can't know every address it answers at; a link to an old address opens in a new tab, which moves to the custom domain. The VAPID subject stays the project's address unless `VAPID_SUBJECT` says otherwise.",
